=== Introduction
--- “Are Saved” or “Being Saved”? [title]
overline: The Doctrine class
heading: “Are Saved” or “Being Saved”?
sub: [[1 Corinthians 1:18]] and [[1 Corinthians 15:2]]
big: 1 Corinthians 1:18 | mark: are saved
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the lesson. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

=== The Greek word
--- σῳζομένοις
kicker: The Greek word
lead: The Greek term σῳζομένοις is a present passive participle (dative masculine plural) from the verb σῴζω (“to save”). It literally means “to those saved.”
p: The present tense in Greek participles can indicate either an ongoing action or a state resulting from a completed action, and the passive voice emphasizes that the action is performed by an external agent—God—rather than by the subjects themselves.

=== Two renderings
--- The King James Bible: “are saved”
kicker: Two renderings
lead: The King James Bible renders σῳζομένοις as “are saved,” using the simple present tense to emphasize a completed action with an assured future state.
callout: This translation aligns with the doctrine of eternal security: salvation is a definitive, irreversible act accomplished by God, and “are saved” presents it as a settled, present reality for the believer.

--- Modern versions: “are being saved”
kicker: Two renderings
lead: Modern versions (NASB, ESV, NIV, etc.) typically translate it as “are being saved,” using the English present progressive.
p: While this reflects one possible aspect of the Greek present tense and highlights ongoing effects, the progressive form can introduce ambiguity, suggesting a process that is incomplete or could potentially be lost.

--- Side by side
kicker: Two renderings
parallel: The King James Bible | Modern versions (NASB, ESV, NIV, etc.)
row: “are saved” | “are being saved”
row: the simple present tense | the English present progressive
row: a completed action with an assured future state | a process that is incomplete or could potentially be lost

=== The two verses
--- The Power of God
kicker: [[1 Corinthians 1:18]]
scripture: 1 Corinthians 1:18 | mark: are saved
p: In 1 Corinthians 1:18 the word of the cross is the power of God precisely to those who stand in the state of having been saved—God’s effective, completed work secures the believer.

--- If Ye Keep in Memory
kicker: [[1 Corinthians 15:2]]
p: In 1 Corinthians 15:2 Paul writes:
scripture: 1 Corinthians 15:2 | mark: ye are saved
p: The “if” clause is not a condition for retaining salvation but the evidence of genuine faith; true believers will hold fast because they are already saved.
refs: Philippians 1:6; John 10:28-29

=== A finished act
--- A Finished Act
lead: The present passive participle here carries its common New Testament sense of a current state resulting from a past, completed action.
p: Scripture repeatedly presents salvation as a finished act that guarantees eternal security for everyone truly in Christ.
refs: Romans 8:30; Ephesians 2:8-9

--- Therefore
p: Therefore, in both 1 Corinthians 1:18 and 15:2 the rendering “are saved” more accurately conveys the assured, completed, and eternally secure state of the believer, while “are being saved,” though grammatically possible, risks undermining that certainty by implying an unfinished or losable process.
callout: The word σῳζομένοις is best understood as describing a permanent present condition of salvation fully accomplished and eternally kept by God.

